---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Guides"
  description="Plain-language guides for local business owners: websites, getting found on Google, and the systems that help a small business run."
>
  <Header />

  <main>
    <Section class="bg-gradient-to-b from-stone-50 to-white dark:from-gray-900 dark:to-gray-950 py-16 sm:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="mb-12">
          <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-white mb-4">
            Guides
          </h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
            Plain-language guides for local business owners: getting found online, websites, and making your tools actually work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-8">
          <!-- Published post -->
          <Card>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6">
              <a href="/guides/website-or-facebook-page/" class="block sm:w-72 sm:flex-shrink-0">
                <img
                  src="/og-website-or-facebook.png"
                  alt="A laptop showing a local business Facebook page next to a phone showing the same business in Google search results"
                  width="1200"
                  height="630"
                  class="w-full rounded-xl border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 hover:opacity-95 transition-opacity"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </a>
              <div class="flex-1">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-3">
                  <Badge variant="accent">Websites</Badge>
                  <Badge variant="accent">Getting Found</Badge>
                </div>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
                  <a href="/guides/website-or-facebook-page/" class="hover:text-accent-600 dark:hover:text-accent-400 transition-colors">
                    Do I Need a Website If I Have a Facebook Page?
                  </a>
                </h2>
                <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 text-sm mb-4">
                  June 26, 2026 &middot; 6 minute read
                </p>
                <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-4">
                  An honest answer for local business owners. A Facebook page is a great start, but on its own it leaves you invisible to a lot of the customers actively trying to find you. Here is where a Facebook-only presence quietly costs you business, what Facebook still does well, and why a simple website works alongside it, backed by the numbers.
                </p>
                <a href="/guides/website-or-facebook-page/" class="text-accent-600 dark:text-accent-400 hover:text-accent-700 dark:hover:text-accent-300 font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-2">
                  Read more
                  <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
                  </svg>
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </Card>

          <!-- Post 1 -->
          <Card>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-3">
              <Badge variant="accent">Operations</Badge>
              <Badge variant="accent">Process Design</Badge>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
              <a href="#" class="hover:text-accent-600 dark:hover:text-accent-400 transition-colors">
                Designing Tiered Support Without Adding Headcount
              </a>
            </h2>
            <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 text-sm mb-4">
              Coming soon
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-4">
              Most support teams get overwhelmed not because they lack people, but because they lack triage. High-priority issues sit in the same queue as routine requests, and everything feels urgent. A tiered support model separates requests by complexity and urgency, routing them to the right people without adding headcount.
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-4">
              This post covers how to design a three-tier model (routine, technical, escalation) with clear escalation criteria, build a knowledge base to reduce repeat questions, and use lightweight ticketing to measure volume and resolution times. The goal: balance workload, resolve high-priority issues faster, and give leadership visibility into support metrics.
            </p>
            <a href="#" class="text-accent-600 dark:text-accent-400 hover:text-accent-700 dark:hover:text-accent-300 font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-2">
              Read more
              <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
              </svg>
            </a>
          </Card>

          <!-- Post 2 -->
          <Card>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-3">
              <Badge variant="accent">Documentation</Badge>
              <Badge variant="accent">Operations</Badge>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
              <a href="#" class="hover:text-accent-600 dark:hover:text-accent-400 transition-colors">
                What Good Operational Documentation Looks Like
              </a>
            </h2>
            <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 text-sm mb-4">
              Coming soon
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-4">
              Bad documentation is worse than no documentation. It gives false confidence, wastes time, and breaks trust when reality doesn't match what's written. Good operational documentation is clear, current, and useful. It helps someone complete a task without asking for help.
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-4">
              This post outlines what makes operational documentation effective: clear ownership, step-by-step instructions with screenshots, version history, and a maintenance schedule. It covers how to structure SOPs, when to use decision trees vs. linear steps, and how to keep docs from getting stale. The goal: documentation that actually gets used.
            </p>
            <a href="#" class="text-accent-600 dark:text-accent-400 hover:text-accent-700 dark:hover:text-accent-300 font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-2">
              Read more
              <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
              </svg>
            </a>
          </Card>
        </div>

        <!-- Coming Soon Message -->
        <div class="mt-12 text-center p-8 bg-accent-50 dark:bg-accent-900/20 rounded-lg">
          <p class="text-lg text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
            More posts coming soon. Writing about revenue operations, Salesforce optimization, and lessons learned from building systems that scale.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
